            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a litter-bearer, then the Latin for a farm housekeeper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: manūmissiō, manūmissiōnis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "manūmissiō, manūmissiōnis — \"the freeing of a slave\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Post manūmissiōnem servus lībertus est. After being set free, the slave is a freedman."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, third declension: manus 'hand' + mittō 'to send' — to let go from the hand. English manumission."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "manūmissiō is built on manūmittō \"to set free\", from manus \"hand, power\" and mittō \"to let go\" (Lewis & Short). English manumission comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
