        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: vindicta, vindictae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vindicta, vindictae — \"the rod of freedom (in freeing a slave); vengeance\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Magistrātus servum vindictā tangit, et servus līber fit. The magistrate touches the slave with the rod, and the slave becomes free."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension. In one way of freeing a slave, he was touched with this rod before a magistrate. Later it also means vengeance; English vindictive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vindicta is built on vindicō \"to lay claim to, set free\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB), from vindex \"a defender\". English vindictive comes from its later sense, vengeance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
